column3:
Conduct a census of the whole congregation of the men of Israel by their clans, by their ancestral households. Number them by name. Count all the males man by man,
NUM 1:3	<p-n>@Numbers 1:3</p-n>	who is twenty years old or older. Count all who can fight as soldiers for Israel. You and Aaron must record the number of men in their armed groups.
NUM 1:4	<p-n>@Numbers 1:4</p-n>	A man from each tribe, a clan head, must serve with you as his tribe's leader. Each leader must lead the men who will fight for his ancestors' household.
NUM 1:5	<p-n>@Numbers 1:5</p-n>	These are the names of the leaders who must fight with you:
NUM 1:6	<p-n>@Numbers 1:6</p-n>	from the tribe of Simeon, Shelumiel son of Zurishaddai;
NUM 1:7	<p-n>@Numbers 1:7</p-n>	from the tribe of Judah, Nahshon son of Amminadab;
NUM 1:8	<p-n>@Numbers 1:8</p-n>	from the tribe of Issachar, Nethanel son of Zuar;
NUM 1:9	<p-n>@Numbers 1:9</p-n>	from the tribe of Zebulun, Eliab son of Helon;
NUM 1:10	<p-n>@Numbers 1:10</p-n>	from the tribe of Ephraim son of Joseph, Elishama son of Ammihud;
NUM 1:11	<p-n>@Numbers 1:11</p-n>	from the tribe of Benjamin, Abidan son of Gideoni;
NUM 1:12	<p-n>@Numbers 1:12</p-n>	from the tribe of Dan, Ahiezer son of Ammishaddai;
NUM 1:13	<p-n>@Numbers 1:13</p-n>	from the tribe of Asher, Pagiel son of Okran;
NUM 1:14	<p-n>@Numbers 1:14</p-n>	from the tribe of Gad, Eliasaph son of Deuel;
NUM 1:15	<p-n>@Numbers 1:15</p-n>	and from the tribe of Naphtali, Ahira son of Enan.

May the day on which I was born perish,
JOB 3:4	<p-n>@Job 3:4</p-n>	May that day be dark!
JOB 3:5	<p-n>@Job 3:5</p-n>	May darkness and the shadow of death claim it for their own.
JOB 3:6	<p-n>@Job 3:6</p-n>	As for that night, may thick darkness seize it.
JOB 3:7	<p-n>@Job 3:7</p-n>	See, may that night be barren;
JOB 3:8	<p-n>@Job 3:8</p-n>	Those who curse the day–may they curse it,
JOB 3:9	<p-n>@Job 3:9</p-n>	May the stars of that day's dawn be dark.
JOB 3:10	<p-n>@Job 3:10</p-n>	because it did not shut up the doors of my mother's womb,
JOB 3:11	<p-n>@Job 3:11</p-n>	Why did I not die when I came out from the womb?
JOB 3:12	<p-n>@Job 3:12</p-n>	Why did her knees welcome me?
JOB 3:13	<p-n>@Job 3:13</p-n>	For now I would have been lying down quietly.
JOB 3:14	<p-n>@Job 3:14</p-n>	with kings and counselors of the earth,
JOB 3:15	<p-n>@Job 3:15</p-n>	Or I would have been lying down with princes who once had gold,
JOB 3:16	<p-n>@Job 3:16</p-n>	Or perhaps I would have been stillborn,
JOB 3:17	<p-n>@Job 3:17</p-n>	There the wicked cease from trouble;
JOB 3:18	<p-n>@Job 3:18</p-n>	There the prisoners are at ease together;
JOB 3:19	<p-n>@Job 3:19</p-n>	Both small and great people are there;
JOB 3:20	<p-n>@Job 3:20</p-n>	Why is light given to him who suffers?
JOB 3:21	<p-n>@Job 3:21</p-n>	to one who longs for death without it coming;
JOB 3:22	<p-n>@Job 3:22</p-n>	Why is light given to one who rejoices very much
JOB 3:23	<p-n>@Job 3:23</p-n>	Why is light given to a man whose way is hidden,
JOB 3:24	<p-n>@Job 3:24</p-n>	For my sighing happens instead of eating;
JOB 3:25	<p-n>@Job 3:25</p-n>	For the thing that I feared has come upon me;
JOB 3:26	<p-n>@Job 3:26</p-n>	I am not at ease, I am not quiet, and I have no rest;
JOB 4:1	<p-n>@Job 4:1</p-n>	Then Eliphaz the Temanite answered and said,
JOB 4:2	<p-n>@Job 4:2</p-n>	If anyone tries to speak with you, will you be impatient?
JOB 4:3	<p-n>@Job 4:3</p-n>	See, you have instructed many;
JOB 4:4	<p-n>@Job 4:4</p-n>	Your words have supported him who was falling;
JOB 4:5	<p-n>@Job 4:5</p-n>	But now trouble has come to you, and you are weary;
JOB 4:6	<p-n>@Job 4:6</p-n>	Is not your fear your confidence,
JOB 4:7	<p-n>@Job 4:7</p-n>	Think about this, please: Who has ever perished when innocent?
JOB 4:8	<p-n>@Job 4:8</p-n>	According to what I have seen, those who plow iniquity
JOB 4:9	<p-n>@Job 4:9</p-n>	By the breath of God they perish;
JOB 4:10	<p-n>@Job 4:10</p-n>	The roaring of the lion, the voice of the fierce lion,
JOB 4:11	<p-n>@Job 4:11</p-n>	The old lion perishes for lack of victims;
JOB 4:12	<p-n>@Job 4:12</p-n>	Now a certain matter was secretly brought to me,
JOB 4:13	<p-n>@Job 4:13</p-n>	Then came thoughts from visions in the night,
JOB 4:14	<p-n>@Job 4:14</p-n>	It was at night when fear and trembling came upon me,
JOB 4:15	<p-n>@Job 4:15</p-n>	Then a spirit passed before my face,
JOB 4:16	<p-n>@Job 4:16</p-n>	The spirit stood still,
JOB 4:17	<p-n>@Job 4:17</p-n>	Can a mortal man be more righteous than God?

Oh, if only my anguish were weighed;
JOB 6:3	<p-n>@Job 6:3</p-n>	For now it would be heavier than the sand of the seas.
JOB 6:4	<p-n>@Job 6:4</p-n>	For the arrows of the Almighty are in me,
JOB 6:5	<p-n>@Job 6:5</p-n>	Does the wild donkey bray in despair when he has grass?
JOB 6:6	<p-n>@Job 6:6</p-n>	Can that which has no taste be eaten without salt?
JOB 6:7	<p-n>@Job 6:7</p-n>	I refuse to touch them;
JOB 6:8	<p-n>@Job 6:8</p-n>	Oh, that I might have my request;
JOB 6:9	<p-n>@Job 6:9</p-n>	that it would please God to crush me once,
JOB 6:10	<p-n>@Job 6:10</p-n>	May this still be my consolation—
JOB 6:11	<p-n>@Job 6:11</p-n>	What is my strength, that I should continue to hope?
JOB 6:12	<p-n>@Job 6:12</p-n>	Is my strength the strength of stones?
JOB 6:13	<p-n>@Job 6:13</p-n>	Is it not true that I have no help in myself,
JOB 6:14	<p-n>@Job 6:14</p-n>	To the person who is about to faint, faithfulness should be shown by his friend;
JOB 6:15	<p-n>@Job 6:15</p-n>	But my brothers have acted as deceitfully to me as a desert streambed,
JOB 6:16	<p-n>@Job 6:16</p-n>	which are darkened because of ice over them,
JOB 6:17	<p-n>@Job 6:17</p-n>	When they thaw out, they vanish;
JOB 6:18	<p-n>@Job 6:18</p-n>	The caravans that travel by their way turn aside for water;
JOB 6:19	<p-n>@Job 6:19</p-n>	Caravans from Tema looked there,
JOB 6:20	<p-n>@Job 6:20</p-n>	They were disappointed because they had been confident of finding water.
JOB 6:21	<p-n>@Job 6:21</p-n>	For now you friends are nothing to me;
JOB 6:22	<p-n>@Job 6:22</p-n>	Did I say to you, 'Give something to me'
JOB 6:23	<p-n>@Job 6:23</p-n>	or, 'Save me from my adversary's hand'
JOB 6:24	<p-n>@Job 6:24</p-n>	Teach me, and I will hold my peace;
JOB 6:25	<p-n>@Job 6:25</p-n>	How painful are upright words!
JOB 6:26	<p-n>@Job 6:26</p-n>	Do you plan to ignore my words,
JOB 6:27	<p-n>@Job 6:27</p-n>	Indeed, you cast lots for a fatherless child,
JOB 6:28	<p-n>@Job 6:28</p-n>	Now, therefore, please look at me,
JOB 6:29	<p-n>@Job 6:29</p-n>	Relent, I beg you; let there be no injustice with you;
JOB 6:30	<p-n>@Job 6:30</p-n>	Is there evil on my tongue?
JOB 7:1	<p-n>@Job 7:1</p-n>	Does not man have hard labor on earth?
JOB 7:2	<p-n>@Job 7:2</p-n>	Like a slave earnestly desires the shadows of evening,
JOB 7:3	<p-n>@Job 7:3</p-n>	so I have been assigned months of emptiness;
JOB 7:4	<p-n>@Job 7:4</p-n>	When I lie down, I say to myself,
JOB 7:5	<p-n>@Job 7:5</p-n>	My flesh is clothed with worms and clods of dust;
JOB 7:6	<p-n>@Job 7:6</p-n>	My days are swifter than a weaver's shuttle;
JOB 7:7	<p-n>@Job 7:7</p-n>	God, call to mind that my life is only a breath;
JOB 7:8	<p-n>@Job 7:8</p-n>	The eye of God, who sees me, will see me no more;
JOB 7:9	<p-n>@Job 7:9</p-n>	As a cloud is consumed and vanishes away,
JOB 7:10	<p-n>@Job 7:10</p-n>	He will return no more to his house;
JOB 7:11	<p-n>@Job 7:11</p-n>	Therefore I will not restrain my mouth;
JOB 7:12	<p-n>@Job 7:12</p-n>	Am I the sea or a sea monster
JOB 7:13	<p-n>@Job 7:13</p-n>	When I say, 'My bed will comfort me,
JOB 7:14	<p-n>@Job 7:14</p-n>	then you frighten me with dreams
JOB 7:15	<p-n>@Job 7:15</p-n>	so that my soul would choose strangling
JOB 7:16	<p-n>@Job 7:16</p-n>	I despise my life; I would not wish to always be alive;
JOB 7:17	<p-n>@Job 7:17</p-n>	What is man that you should pay attention to him,
JOB 7:18	<p-n>@Job 7:18</p-n>	that you should observe him every morning
JOB 7:19	<p-n>@Job 7:19</p-n>	How long will it be before you look away from me,
JOB 7:20	<p-n>@Job 7:20</p-n>	Even if I have sinned, what would that do to you,
JOB 7:21	<p-n>@Job 7:21</p-n>	Why do you not pardon my transgression
JOB 8:1	<p-n>@Job 8:1</p-n>	Then Bildad the Shuhite answered and said,
JOB 8:2	<p-n>@Job 8:2</p-n>	How long will you say these things?

No doubt you are the people;
JOB 12:3	<p-n>@Job 12:3</p-n>	But I have understanding as well as you;
JOB 12:4	<p-n>@Job 12:4</p-n>	I am something for my neighbor to laugh at—
JOB 12:5	<p-n>@Job 12:5</p-n>	In the thought of someone who is at ease, there is contempt for misfortune;
JOB 12:6	<p-n>@Job 12:6</p-n>	The tents of robbers prosper,
JOB 12:7	<p-n>@Job 12:7</p-n>	But now ask the animals, and they will teach you;
JOB 12:8	<p-n>@Job 12:8</p-n>	Or speak to the earth, and it will teach you;
JOB 12:9	<p-n>@Job 12:9</p-n>	Which animal among all these does not know
JOB 12:10	<p-n>@Job 12:10</p-n>	In his hand is the life of every living thing
JOB 12:11	<p-n>@Job 12:11</p-n>	Does not the ear test words
JOB 12:12	<p-n>@Job 12:12</p-n>	With aged men is wisdom;
JOB 12:13	<p-n>@Job 12:13</p-n>	With God are wisdom and might;
JOB 12:14	<p-n>@Job 12:14</p-n>	See, he breaks things down, and they cannot be built again;
JOB 12:15	<p-n>@Job 12:15</p-n>	See, if he withholds the waters, they dry up;
JOB 12:16	<p-n>@Job 12:16</p-n>	With him are strength and wisdom;
JOB 12:17	<p-n>@Job 12:17</p-n>	He leads counselors away barefoot in sorrow;
JOB 12:18	<p-n>@Job 12:18</p-n>	He removes the bond of kings;
JOB 12:19	<p-n>@Job 12:19</p-n>	He leads priests away barefoot in sorrow
JOB 12:20	<p-n>@Job 12:20</p-n>	He removes the speech of those who had been trusted
JOB 12:21	<p-n>@Job 12:21</p-n>	He pours contempt upon princes
JOB 12:22	<p-n>@Job 12:22</p-n>	He reveals the deep things of darkness
JOB 12:23	<p-n>@Job 12:23</p-n>	He makes nations strong, and he also destroys them;
JOB 12:24	<p-n>@Job 12:24</p-n>	He takes away understanding from the leaders of the people of the earth;
JOB 12:25	<p-n>@Job 12:25</p-n>	They grope in the dark without light;
JOB 13:1	<p-n>@Job 13:1</p-n>	See, my eye has seen all this;
JOB 13:2	<p-n>@Job 13:2</p-n>	What you know, the same I also know;
JOB 13:3	<p-n>@Job 13:3</p-n>	However, I would rather speak with the Almighty;
JOB 13:4	<p-n>@Job 13:4</p-n>	But you whitewash the truth with lies;
JOB 13:5	<p-n>@Job 13:5</p-n>	Oh, that you would altogether hold your peace!
JOB 13:6	<p-n>@Job 13:6</p-n>	Hear now my own reasoning;
JOB 13:7	<p-n>@Job 13:7</p-n>	Will you speak unrighteousness for God,
JOB 13:8	<p-n>@Job 13:8</p-n>	Will you show him partiality?
JOB 13:9	<p-n>@Job 13:9</p-n>	Will it be good for you when he examines you?
JOB 13:10	<p-n>@Job 13:10</p-n>	He would surely reprove you
JOB 13:11	<p-n>@Job 13:11</p-n>	Will not his majesty terrify you,
JOB 13:12	<p-n>@Job 13:12</p-n>	Your memorable sayings are proverbs made of ashes;
JOB 13:13	<p-n>@Job 13:13</p-n>	Hold your peace, let me alone, so that I may speak,
JOB 13:14	<p-n>@Job 13:14</p-n>	I will take my own flesh in my teeth;
JOB 13:15	<p-n>@Job 13:15</p-n>	See, if he kills me, I will have no hope left;
JOB 13:16	<p-n>@Job 13:16</p-n>	This will be the reason for my deliverance,
JOB 13:17	<p-n>@Job 13:17</p-n>	God, listen carefully to my speech;
JOB 13:18	<p-n>@Job 13:18</p-n>	See now, I have set my defense in order;
JOB 13:19	<p-n>@Job 13:19</p-n>	Who is the one who would argue against me in court?
JOB 13:20	<p-n>@Job 13:20</p-n>	God, do only two things for me,
JOB 13:21	<p-n>@Job 13:21</p-n>	withdraw your oppressive hand from me,
JOB 13:22	<p-n>@Job 13:22</p-n>	Then call me, and I will answer;
JOB 13:23	<p-n>@Job 13:23</p-n>	How many are my iniquities and sins?
JOB 13:24	<p-n>@Job 13:24</p-n>	Why do you hide your face from me
JOB 13:25	<p-n>@Job 13:25</p-n>	Will you cause a driven leaf to tremble?
JOB 13:26	<p-n>@Job 13:26</p-n>	For you write down bitter things against me;
JOB 13:27	<p-n>@Job 13:27</p-n>	You also put my feet in the stocks;
JOB 13:28	<p-n>@Job 13:28</p-n>	although I am like a rotten thing that wastes away,
JOB 14:1	<p-n>@Job 14:1</p-n>	Man, who is born of woman,
JOB 14:2	<p-n>@Job 14:2</p-n>	He sprouts from the ground like a flower and is cut down;
JOB 14:3	<p-n>@Job 14:3</p-n>	Do you look at any of these?
JOB 14:4	<p-n>@Job 14:4</p-n>	Who can bring something clean out of something unclean? 
JOB 14:5	<p-n>@Job 14:5</p-n>	Man's days are determined.
JOB 14:6	<p-n>@Job 14:6</p-n>	Look away from him that he may rest,
JOB 14:7	<p-n>@Job 14:7</p-n>	There can be hope for a tree;
JOB 14:8	<p-n>@Job 14:8</p-n>	Though its root grows old in the earth,
JOB 14:9	<p-n>@Job 14:9</p-n>	yet even if it only smells water, it will bud
JOB 14:10	<p-n>@Job 14:10</p-n>	But man dies; he becomes weak;
JOB 14:11	<p-n>@Job 14:11</p-n>	As water disappears from a lake,
JOB 14:12	<p-n>@Job 14:12</p-n>	so people lie down and do not rise again.
JOB 14:13	<p-n>@Job 14:13</p-n>	Oh, that you would hide me away in Sheol away from troubles,
JOB 14:14	<p-n>@Job 14:14</p-n>	If a man dies, will he live again?
JOB 14:15	<p-n>@Job 14:15</p-n>	You would call, and I would answer you.
JOB 14:16	<p-n>@Job 14:16</p-n>	You would number and care for my footsteps;
JOB 14:17	<p-n>@Job 14:17</p-n>	My transgression would be sealed up in a bag;
JOB 14:18	<p-n>@Job 14:18</p-n>	But even mountains fall and crumble to nothing;
JOB 14:19	<p-n>@Job 14:19</p-n>	the waters wear down the stones;
JOB 14:20	<p-n>@Job 14:20</p-n>	You forever defeat him, and he passes away;
JOB 14:21	<p-n>@Job 14:21</p-n>	If his sons are honored, he does not know it;
JOB 14:22	<p-n>@Job 14:22</p-n>	He feels only the pain of his own body,
JOB 15:1	<p-n>@Job 15:1</p-n>	Then Eliphaz the Temanite answered and said,
JOB 15:2	<p-n>@Job 15:2</p-n>	Should a wise man answer with useless knowledge

I have heard many such things;
JOB 16:3	<p-n>@Job 16:3</p-n>	Will useless words ever have an end?
JOB 16:4	<p-n>@Job 16:4</p-n>	I also could speak as you do,
JOB 16:5	<p-n>@Job 16:5</p-n>	I would strengthen you with my mouth,
JOB 16:6	<p-n>@Job 16:6</p-n>	If I speak, my grief is not lessened;
JOB 16:7	<p-n>@Job 16:7</p-n>	But now, God, you have made me weary;
JOB 16:8	<p-n>@Job 16:8</p-n>	You have made me dry up, 
JOB 16:9	<p-n>@Job 16:9</p-n>	God has torn me in his wrath and persecuted me;
JOB 16:10	<p-n>@Job 16:10</p-n>	People have gaped with open mouth at me;
JOB 16:11	<p-n>@Job 16:11</p-n>	God hands me over to ungodly people,
JOB 16:12	<p-n>@Job 16:12</p-n>	I was at ease, and he broke me apart.
JOB 16:13	<p-n>@Job 16:13</p-n>	His archers surround me all around;
JOB 16:14	<p-n>@Job 16:14</p-n>	He smashes through my wall again and again;
JOB 16:15	<p-n>@Job 16:15</p-n>	I have sewn sackcloth on my skin;
JOB 16:16	<p-n>@Job 16:16</p-n>	My face is red with weeping;
JOB 16:17	<p-n>@Job 16:17</p-n>	although there is no violence in my hands,
JOB 16:18	<p-n>@Job 16:18</p-n>	Earth, do not cover up my blood;
JOB 16:19	<p-n>@Job 16:19</p-n>	Even now, see, my witness is in heaven;
JOB 16:20	<p-n>@Job 16:20</p-n>	My friends scoff at me,
JOB 16:21	<p-n>@Job 16:21</p-n>	I ask for that witness in heaven to argue for this man with God
JOB 16:22	<p-n>@Job 16:22</p-n>	For when a few years have passed,
JOB 17:1	<p-n>@Job 17:1</p-n>	My spirit is broken, and my days are over;
JOB 17:2	<p-n>@Job 17:2</p-n>	Surely there are mockers with me;
JOB 17:3	<p-n>@Job 17:3</p-n>	Give now a pledge, be a guarantee for me with yourself;
JOB 17:4	<p-n>@Job 17:4</p-n>	For you, God, have kept their hearts from understanding;
JOB 17:5	<p-n>@Job 17:5</p-n>	He who denounces his friends for a reward,
JOB 17:6	<p-n>@Job 17:6</p-n>	But he has made me a byword of the people;
JOB 17:7	<p-n>@Job 17:7</p-n>	My eye is also dim because of sorrow;
JOB 17:8	<p-n>@Job 17:8</p-n>	Upright men will be appalled by this;
JOB 17:9	<p-n>@Job 17:9</p-n>	The righteous man will keep to his way;
JOB 17:10	<p-n>@Job 17:10</p-n>	But as for you all, come on now;
JOB 17:11	<p-n>@Job 17:11</p-n>	My days are past; my plans are shattered,
JOB 17:12	<p-n>@Job 17:12</p-n>	These people, these mockers, change the night into day;
JOB 17:13	<p-n>@Job 17:13</p-n>	If the only home I hope for is Sheol;
JOB 17:14	<p-n>@Job 17:14</p-n>	and if I have said to the pit, 'You are my father,'
JOB 17:15	<p-n>@Job 17:15</p-n>	where then is my hope?
JOB 17:16	<p-n>@Job 17:16</p-n>	Will hope go down with me to the gates of Sheol
JOB 18:1	<p-n>@Job 18:1</p-n>	Then Bildad the Shuhite answered and said,
JOB 18:2	<p-n>@Job 18:2</p-n>	When will you stop your talk?

My thoughts make me answer quickly
JOB 20:3	<p-n>@Job 20:3</p-n>	I hear a rebuke that dishonors me,
JOB 20:4	<p-n>@Job 20:4</p-n>	Do you not know this fact from ancient times,
JOB 20:5	<p-n>@Job 20:5</p-n>	the triumph of a wicked man is short,
JOB 20:6	<p-n>@Job 20:6</p-n>	Though his height reaches up to the heavens,
JOB 20:7	<p-n>@Job 20:7</p-n>	yet such a person will perish forever like his own dung;
JOB 20:8	<p-n>@Job 20:8</p-n>	He will fly away like a dream and will not be found;
JOB 20:9	<p-n>@Job 20:9</p-n>	The eye that saw him will see him no more;
JOB 20:10	<p-n>@Job 20:10</p-n>	His children will apologize to poor people;
JOB 20:11	<p-n>@Job 20:11</p-n>	His bones are full of youthful strength,
JOB 20:12	<p-n>@Job 20:12</p-n>	Although wickedness is sweet in his mouth,
JOB 20:13	<p-n>@Job 20:13</p-n>	although he holds it there and does not let it go
JOB 20:14	<p-n>@Job 20:14</p-n>	the food in his intestines turns bitter;
JOB 20:15	<p-n>@Job 20:15</p-n>	He swallows down riches, but he will vomit them up again;
JOB 20:16	<p-n>@Job 20:16</p-n>	He will suck the poison of asps;
JOB 20:17	<p-n>@Job 20:17</p-n>	He will not enjoy the streams,
